# Claude Code
# The configuration is tracked — `.claude/settings.json` carries the session-start hook, and a
# hook nobody can review is a hook nobody can correct. The session briefs are not: they are a
# per-machine handoff, they name work in progress rather than decisions, and the decisions that
# outlive a session belong in `docs/audit/` where the record already keeps them.
.claude/sessions/
# Ignored here and not only in a personal global ignore file: on any other checkout the first
# permission grant writes this, `git status` sees it, and the session-start banner then reports
# an uncommitted change at every startup — a standing false finding in the one instrument whose
# value is that a finding means something.
.claude/settings.local.json
# And the worktrees a subagent runs in. Two harms, both tripped on 2026-09-11 rather than
# predicted: `python -m tools.entry_state` called the tree dirty for the whole life of a
# review agent — the same standing false finding the line above exists to prevent, one
# directory over — and a `git add -A` during that window staged the worktree as an **embedded
# git repository**, which git warns about and commits anyway. `0008` §5 records this class
# arriving in the submodules; this is it at the root.
.claude/worktrees/

# Kept after the identifier sweep was retired on 2026-09-11 (`0010` §3.2), and kept
# deliberately rather than by oversight: a working copy of this name may still sit at this
# root, and without the pattern `git status` would report it forever and `tools/entry_state`
# would call the tree dirty at every session start — a standing false finding in the one
# instrument whose value is that a finding means something. The pattern costs nothing and
# nothing may ever commit a file of this name.
audit-identifiers.local
